=== task: multi medium
title: The stack version
prompt: Choose **every** true statement about the stack version of preorder.
* Every node is pushed once and popped once :: Right. So it's O(n).
* It visits the nodes in the same order as the recursive version :: Right. As long as the right child is pushed first.
* The stack stays small: about one waiting node for each level of the current path :: Right. O(h), like the call stack of the recursive version.
- It needs a queue instead of a stack :: A queue would give level order.
- Pushing the left child first still gives preorder :: That gives node, right, left.
- It takes more steps than the recursive version: O(n log n) :: Both are O(n).
hint: How many times does each node go on and come off the stack?
hint: What waits on the stack: whole levels, or right children along the current path?
right: O(n) time, the same order as recursion, and O(h) waiting: the explicit stack does what the call stack did.
wrong: Each node goes on and off once (O(n)), the order matches recursion when the right child is pushed first, and only right siblings along the current path wait (O(h)).

